<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Zhenzong of Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/968">968</a>–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Zhao Heng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Zhenzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was the third emperor of the Northern Song dynasty, reigning from 997 to 1022. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> invasion of 1004 he concluded the Treaty of Chanyuan (1005), which bought lasting peace with annual payments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. His reign saw the introduction of quick-ripening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and lavish state rituals such as the feng and shan sacrifices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> in 1008.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhao Heng was born on 23 December 968, the third son of Emperor Taizong. His personal name was first Zhao Dechang, then Zhao Yuanxiu in 983, Zhao Yuankan in 986, and finally Zhao Heng in 995. He became the third emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in 997, and was succeeded at the end of his reign by his sixth son, Emperor Renzong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign is noted for the consolidation of power and for prosperity, with the military further reinforced. It also began a policy towards the Khitan-led Liao dynasty in the north that ended in humiliation. In 1004 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> made war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and Zhenzong led his army against them. Despite early successes he concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Chanyuan Treaty</a> in 1005. It brought more than a century of peace, but the Song accepted a position inferior to the Liao and agreed to pay each year 100,000 ounces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and over 200,000 bolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. This admission of inferiority troubled later Song foreign relations, and the payments slowly drained the treasury.</p><p>In 1011 and 1012 he ordered 30,000 bushels of quick-maturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> seed shipped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> basin to improve agriculture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> during his reign. In 1013 officials were evaluated by tax targets, usually set as averages of the highest and lowest collections of three years. Zhenzong stressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> at court. During his reign the so-called Heavenly Texts, which glorified the Zhao family, were allegedly discovered. Imperial sacrifices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> followed, and from 1013 to 1015 he issued decrees deifying the Jade Emperor as the highest ruler of Heaven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1020 Zhenzong fell ill and could no longer handle the affairs of state. His wife, Empress Liu, was already established as the power behind the throne, and acted unofficially as regent for the last two years of his life. He died on 23 March 1022, and Renzong succeeded him, with real power remaining in the hands of Empress Liu, who became empress dowager.</p><p>Artefacts from the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties, some of which had belonged to Zhenzong, were excavated and came into the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> general Ma Hongkui, who did not publicise them. After his death his wife took them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1971 and gave them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, who turned them over to the National Palace Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Zhenzong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Emperor <b>Zhenzong</b>, early-ripening, drought-tolerant Champa rice was introduced from central Vietnam; it allowed double cropping in southern China and supported the period's rapid population growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.8,114.35&amp;year=1003" aria-label="Kaifeng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7451 904 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Zhenzong of Song"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7901 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.8,114.35&amp;year=1003">What was here in 1003? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
